  "chatSessions": [
    {
      "id": "chat-201",
      "patientId": "pat-1",
      "title": "Low-Grade Fever & Sore Throat Inquiry",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-30T09:45:00Z",
      "messages": [
        {
          "id": "msg-1",
          "role": "user",
          "text": "I have had a mild fever and sore throat for the past 2 days. What could be causing this?",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-30T09:44:10Z",
          "language": "English"
        },
        {
          "id": "msg-2",
          "role": "model",
          "text": "I understand how uncomfortable a persistent fever and sore throat can feel. Based on what you have shared, this may be related to a few common possibilities:\n\n**Common Possible Causes:**\n- **Viral Upper Respiratory Tract Infection (Common Cold or Seasonal Flu):** Viruses affecting the nose and throat are the most frequent trigger for mild fever accompanied by throat irritation.\n- **Viral or Bacterial Pharyngitis:** Inflammation of the back of the throat (pharynx) triggered by airborne droplets or environmental irritants.\n- **Allergic Rhinitis with Post-Nasal Drip:** Allergens such as dust or pollen can cause mucus to trickle down the back of the throat, leading to soreness and mild systemic fatigue.\n\n**Plain-Language Explanation:**\nA fever is your immune system's natural defense response as it works to clear an infection or inflammation, while a sore throat happens when the delicate lining of your throat becomes swollen and irritated. Because many mild viral and bacterial conditions look similar early on, a clinical examination helps determine the exact cause.",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-30T09:44:25Z",
          "language": "English",
          "isEmergency": false,
          "detectedSymptoms": "Mild fever and sore throat for 2 days"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
